    <!-- EDITORIAL COMPREHENSIVE OVERVIEW -->
    <section class="card">
      <h2 class="section-title">Comprehensive Guide to the 8th Central Pay Commission</h2>
      <p class="section-desc">Historical evolution, inflationary economic metrics, and compensation restructuring for Central Government employees.</p>
      
      <div class="editorial-prose">
        <p>
          The <strong>8th Central Pay Commission (CPC)</strong> represents the upcoming statutory remuneration revision framework designed by the Government of India to recalibrate the earnings, allowances, post-retirement annuities, and operational benefits for over 4.8 million regular central government employees and upwards of 6.7 million pensioners. Established on a decennial (10-year) precedent since post-independence India, pay commissions play an essential role in bridging the gap between historical wage increments and ongoing retail inflation measured through the Consumer Price Index for Industrial Workers (CPI-IW).
        </p>
        <p>
          With the implementation of the 7th Central Pay Commission taking effect on January 1, 2016, central employees and pensioner federations nationwide have focused on 2026 as the scheduled threshold for the commencement of the 8th CPC. Over the preceding decade, key macroeconomic indicators have shifted significantly. The continuous compounding of retail inflation has led Dearness Allowance (DA) and Dearness Relief (DR) to reach and exceed the critical 50% milestone. Under central service rules, crossing the 50% DA mark triggers institutional re-examinations of critical allowances, such as House Rent Allowance (HRA), Children Education Allowance (CEA), and Transport Allowance (TA).
        </p>

        <h3>Core Objectives of Pay Revisions in Public Service</h3>
        <p>
          The structural formulation of the 8th Pay Commission is expected to pursue four primary administrative mandates:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Real Wage Preservation:</strong> Incorporating cumulative inflation by merging accumulated Dearness Allowance directly into the basic pay structure.</li>
          <li><strong>Compression Ratio Balancing:</strong> Addressing the disparity between entry-level Grade Pay (Level 1) and senior bureaucratic executive echelons (Level 18, Cabinet Secretary scale).</li>
          <li><strong>Simplification of Matrix Progression:</strong> Refining the Level 1 to 18 Pay Matrix cells introduced under the 7th CPC to eliminate career stagnation and anomalous promotional pay ceilings.</li>
          <li><strong>Parity in Pensioner Entitlements:</strong> Ensuring retired defence personnel, civilian veterans, and family pensioners receive equitable pension adjustments matching active duty increments.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 8TH PAY COMMISSION SALARY CALCULATOR -->
    <section id="salary-calculator">
      <div class="card">
        <h2 class="section-title">8th Pay Commission Salary Calculator</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">Interactive computational tool to project revised monthly basic pay, HRA, and gross salary based on varying fitment multipliers.</p>

        <form id="mainSalaryForm">
          <div class="calc-inputs-grid">
            <!-- Level Quick-Select Dropdown -->
            <div class="form-group">
              <label for="calcQuickLevel">Select Pay Matrix Level (Preset)</label>
              <select id="calcQuickLevel">
                <option value="" disabled selected>-- Choose Pay Level --</option>
                {payMatrixData.map((row) => (
                  <option value={row.entry7th}>{row.level} ({row.gp}) - Min ₹{row.entry7th.toLocaleString('en-IN')}</option>
                ))}
              </select>
            </div>

            <div class="form-group">
              <label for="calc7thBasic">7th CPC Current Basic Pay (₹)</label>
              <input type="number" id="calc7thBasic" value="18000" min="18000" step="100" required />
            </div>

            <div class="form-group">
              <label for="calcFitment">Fitment Factor Multiplier</label>
              <select id="calcFitment">
                <option value="1.92">1.92x (Conservative Benchmark)</option>
                <option value="2.10">2.10x (Balanced Projection)</option>
                <option value="2.25" selected>2.25x (Moderate Baseline)</option>
                <option value="2.57">2.57x (7th CPC Equivalent)</option>
                <option value="2.86">2.86x (Federation Demand)</option>
              </select>
            </div>

            <div class="form-group">
              <label for="calcHra">City Category (HRA Classification)</label>
              <select id="calcHra">
                <option value="0.30" selected>X Class (30% Metro: Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai, etc.)</option>
                <option value="0.20">Y Class (20% Tier-2 Cities)</option>
                <option value="0.10">Z Class (10% Tier-3 Rural/Small Towns)</option>
              </select>
            </div>
          </div>
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; width: 100%;">Calculate 8th CPC Projected Salary</button>
        </form>

        <div id="salaryResults" class="calc-output">
          <div class="calc-metrics-grid">
            <div class="calc-metric-card">
              <div class="metric-label">Projected 8th CPC Basic</div>
              <div class="metric-value" id="resRevisedBasic">₹0</div>
            </div>
            <div class="calc-metric-card">
              <div class="metric-label">Estimated Revised HRA</div>
              <div class="metric-value" id="resRevisedHRA">₹0</div>
            </div>
            <div class="calc-metric-card accent">
              <div class="metric-label">Estimated Monthly Gross</div>
              <div class="metric-value" id="resGrossTotal">₹0</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="editorial-prose" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <h3>Mathematical Methodology of Salary Projections</h3>
          <p>
            The calculation model follows standard statutory precedent: <br />
            <code>Revised Basic Salary = Current 7th CPC Basic × Fitment Factor Multiplier</code><br />
            Because a new Pay Commission absorbs preceding dearness allowances into the revised base pay, the Dearness Allowance rate resets to 0% on the date of effect. The allowance cycle subsequently rebuilds biannually (in January and July) based on twelve-month moving averages of the AICPI-IW index.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FITMENT FACTOR DEEP DIVE -->
    <section id="fitment-factor">
      <div class="card">
        <h2 class="section-title">Fitment Factor: The Core Multiplier Explained</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">Analyze how distinct multiplier scenarios reshape basic salaries across lower, middle, and senior administrative cadres.</p>

        <div class="factor-buttons-container">
          <div class="factor-pill-btn" data-factor="1.92">1.92x (Conservative)</div>
          <div class="factor-pill-btn" data-factor="2.10">2.10x</div>
          <div class="factor-pill-btn active" data-factor="2.25">2.25x (Baseline)</div>
          <div class="factor-pill-btn" data-factor="2.57">2.57x (7th CPC Baseline)</div>
          <div class="factor-pill-btn" data-factor="2.86">2.86x (Union Target)</div>
        </div>

        <div class="table-responsive">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Multiplier Scenario</th>
                <th>Level 1 (MTS: ₹18,000)</th>
                <th>Level 6 (Inspector: ₹35,400)</th>
                <th>Level 10 (Gazetted: ₹56,100)</th>
                <th>Level 14 (Joint Sec: ₹1,44,200)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong id="displayFactor">2.25x Multiplier Selected</strong></td>
                <td id="factorLvl1">₹40,500</td>
                <td id="factorLvl6">₹79,650</td>
                <td id="factorLvl10">₹1,26,225</td>
                <td id="factorLvl14">₹3,24,450</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="editorial-prose" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <h3>Historical Progression of Pay Commission Multipliers</h3>
          <p>
            To appreciate the function of the fitment factor, one must observe its historical trajectory. The 5th CPC utilized a base formula that integrated existing pay scales with 40% fitment weightage. The 6th CPC (2006) introduced running pay bands coupled with Grade Pay and utilized a multiplier factor of approximately 1.86x. The 7th CPC (2016) made a major departure by disbanding the Grade Pay structure altogether, replacing it with the Level 1 to 18 Pay Matrix and establishing a uniform fitment multiplier of 2.57x.
          </p>
          <p>
            Under the expected 8th CPC deliberations, staff side confederations including the National Council (JCM) have advocated for a factor between 2.86x and 3.25x. Their rationale rests on the Aykroyd formula of minimum wage computation, factoring in nutrition, clothing, housing, and social expenditure benchmarks. Conversely, macroeconomic analysts suggest a factor between 1.92x and 2.28x to ensure fiscal deficit sustainability for the exchequer.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 8TH CPC PAY MATRIX (LEVEL 1 TO LEVEL 18) -->
    <section id="pay-matrix">
      <div class="card">
        <h2 class="section-title">8th CPC Pay Matrix: Levels 1 to 18</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">Comprehensive comparative grid reflecting current 7th CPC starting pay versus projected 8th CPC pay at 2.25x and 2.57x fitment factors.</p>

        <div class="table-responsive">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Pay Level</th>
                <th>Equivalent Grade Pay</th>
                <th>Designation Cadre</th>
                <th>7th CPC Base</th>
                <th>8th CPC (2.25x Baseline)</th>
                <th>8th CPC (2.57x Parity)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {payMatrixData.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>{row.level}</strong></td>
                  <td>{row.gp}</td>
                  <td>{row.post}</td>
                  <td>₹{row.entry7th.toLocaleString('en-IN')}</td>
                  <td><strong>₹{Math.round(row.entry7th * 2.25).toLocaleString('en-IN')}</strong></td>
                  <td><strong style="color: var(--primary-accent);">₹{Math.round(row.entry7th * 2.57).toLocaleString('en-IN')}</strong></td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="editorial-prose" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <h3>The Structure and Mechanics of the Pay Matrix</h3>
          <p>
            The Pay Matrix simplifies the civil and defence salary framework by grouping positions into 18 distinct vertical levels. Horizontally, each level comprises upwards of 30 to 40 sequential index cells representing annual increments (standardized at approximately 3% per year). Under the 8th Pay Commission, this matrix system will be preserved while updating the base cell values to prevent stagnation in long-tenure posts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SALARY BY PAY LEVEL LOOKUP -->
    <section>
      <div class="card">
        <h2 class="section-title">Pay Matrix Level Lookup</h2>
        <p class="section-desc">Click any level badge below to quickly inspect starting salary transformations across cadres.</p>
        
        <div class="level-badge-grid">
